<section>
<p>Slide 3</p>
<h2>Where the Real Problem Is</h2>

<p>Current AI-enabled tools already provide:</p>
<ul>
<li>Data summarization</li>
<li>Suggestions and recommendations</li>
<li>Anomaly detection</li>
<li>Alerts and notifications</li>
</ul>

<p>Yet the operational workflow still looks like:</p>
<p>Signal → Search → Interpret → Find owner → Coordinate → Execute → Follow up → Report</p>

<p>The remaining human effort is concentrated in:</p>
<ul>
<li>Deciding what matters now</li>
<li>Reconstructing context across systems</li>
<li>Finding the right owner and stakeholders</li>
<li>Turning insight into communication and action</li>
<li>Following the issue until it is resolved</li>
</ul>

<p>Core opportunity</p>
<p><strong>The gap is not better information retrieval. The gap is operational follow-through.</strong></p>
</section>

<hr>

<!-- Slide 4 -->

<section>
<p>Slide 4</p>
<h2>From Information Retrieval to Work Execution</h2>

<p>Today</p>
<p>Alert → Open multiple systems → Search emails → Reconstruct context → Find owner → Draft communication → Chase responses → Update tracker</p>

<p>With an AI teammate</p>
<p>Signal → Contextualized task → Prioritized action → Draft communication → Human approval → Track to closure</p>

<p>What changes</p>
<ul>
<li>Information is organized around the task, not the source system.</li>
<li>The planner spends less time reconstructing context.</li>
<li>Communication becomes part of the workflow rather than a separate task.</li>
<li>Follow-up becomes visible and trackable.</li>
</ul>

<p>What does not change</p>
<p>The planner remains accountable for consequential business decisions.</p>
</section>

