<section>
<p>Slide 9</p>
<h2>Why Start with the Supply/Demand Planner?</h2>

<p>Based on the assumptions available today, SDP offers the strongest starting point because:</p>

<ul>
<li>High-frequency operational decisions create a large recurring opportunity.</li>
<li>The core workflow can be standardized enough to support an initial AI-assisted loop.</li>
<li>Relevant operational data is more likely to be available in structured internal systems.</li>
<li>The consequences of individual recommendation errors can remain within a human approval boundary in the MVP.</li>
<li>The underlying capabilities — ownership, task context, prioritization, recommendation, communication, and follow-up — are highly reusable.</li>
</ul>

<p>Why not start elsewhere?</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Procurement / GSM:</strong> more external data and heterogeneous communication patterns.</li>
<li><strong>Logistics:</strong> multiple internal and external event sources increase integration complexity.</li>
<li><strong>Manufacturing interface:</strong> partner-specific processes reduce workflow standardization.</li>
<li><strong>Executives:</strong> the executive experience depends on reliable upstream operational intelligence.</li>
</ul>

<p>Decision</p>
<p><strong>SDP is the first wedge, not the final target.</strong></p>
</section>

